---
import { OFFICIAL_SOURCES } from '../../config/site';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const FAQ_ITEMS = [
  {
    question: 'Should a contractor list model numbers and the AHRI certificate on an HVAC quote?',
    answer:
      'Yes. Exact outdoor condenser and indoor coil model numbers plus the AHRI Certified Reference Number let you confirm DOE 10 CFR 430.32 SEER2 compliance and verify rebate eligibility before paying a deposit.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How much do omitted permits, line sets, and plenum transitions add to a cheap quote?',
    answer:
      'Leaving permits, a new copper line set, disconnect wiring, and sheet-metal transitions off an initial bid often shifts $1,000 to $1,800 into mid-install change orders or leaves code compliance unverified.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why does an R-22 replacement bid usually need a new copper line set?',
    answer:
      'R-22 equipment used mineral oil while newer R-410A, R-454B, and R-32 systems use POE synthetic oil. Residual oil or undersized suction piping can damage the replacement compressor unless replaced or properly flushed.',
  },
] as const;

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: FAQ_ITEMS.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.question,
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: item.answer,
    },
  })),
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How to Read an HVAC Quote (Line Items & Red Flags) | HVAC Second Opinion"
  description="Learn how to read an HVAC quote line by line, spot missing permits, disposal, line sets, and thermostat work, and avoid vague contractor bid traps."
  structuredData={faqSchema}
>
  <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Calculator</a> / <a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / <span>How to Read an HVAC Quote</span>
  </nav>

  <article class="prose">
    <h1>How to Read an HVAC Quote</h1>
    <p class="intro-lead">
      Two HVAC contractors can price the exact same 3-ton heat pump <strong>$1,500 apart ($7,900 vs. $9,400)</strong> while delivering wildly different scopes of work. One quote covers city mechanical permits, a fresh copper line set, and proper EPA recovery; the other is a vague total that triggers $1,700 in change orders once your old condenser sits in the driveway.
    </p>

    <h2>What a Complete Replacement Quote Should List</h2>
    <p>
      An auditable installation bid reads like a parts-and-labor manifest rather than a brochure. Before comparing prices, confirm every bid includes these six core items:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Exact outdoor and indoor model numbers:</strong> Require manufacturer, tonnage, and the AHRI Certified Reference Number to verify compliance with <a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.doeSeer2} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. DOE regional minimums (10 CFR 430.32)</a>—13.4 SEER2 North, or 14.3 / 13.8 SEER2 Southeast and Southwest. See <a href="/guides/seer2-explained/">our SEER2 guide</a> for tier breakdowns.</li>
      <li><strong>Refrigerant type named on the bid:</strong> Under the <a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.epaAim} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EPA AIM Act phasedown</a>, new residential split systems transition from R-410A to lower-GWP A2L fluids such as R-454B or R-32. Your contract should name the exact refrigerant.</li>
      <li><strong>Installation materials and safety switches:</strong> Look for condenser pad leveling, condensate trap and float switch, 240V disconnect whip, and sealed plenum transitions.</li>
      <li><strong>Parts vs. labor warranties separated:</strong> Manufacturer parts warranties (typically 10 years registered) exclude refrigerant and technician labor. Verify written contractor labor years separately.</li>
      <li><strong>Documented load sizing:</strong> Written confirmation of an ACCA Manual J load calculation rather than guessing by square footage (see <a href="/guides/how-to-vet-hvac-contractor/">how to vet a contractor</a>).</li>
      <li><strong>Milestone payment schedule:</strong> Payments tied to deposit, installation completion, and passed municipal inspection rather than 100% upfront.</li>
    </ul>

    <figure class="manual-figure">
      <img
        src="/images/fig-02-quote-anatomy.svg"
        alt="Technical diagram linking indoor air handler, copper line set, thermostat wire, and outdoor condenser with five commonly omitted HVAC quote items."
        width="820"
        height="340"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Figure 02: Five line items most often omitted from low lump-sum replacement quotes ($7,900 base + $1,700 missed scope = $9,600 real cost).
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Five Items Most Commonly Missing From HVAC Quotes</h2>
    <p>
      When a bid lands noticeably lower than competing proposals, check these five scope lines first—omitting them rarely eliminates the work:
    </p>

    <div tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Commonly omitted HVAC quote line items" class="table-wrapper">
      <table class="estimate-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Omitted Line Item</th>
            <th>Why It Matters Technically</th>
            <th>What to Demand in Writing</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Municipal Permits &amp; Inspection</th>
            <td>Most US jurisdictions require mechanical and electrical permits; unpermitted swaps create home-sale and insurance issues.</td>
            <td>“Contractor pulls and pays for municipal permits and schedules final inspection.”</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">EPA 608 Recovery &amp; Disposal</th>
            <td>Under <a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.epaR22} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EPA Clean Air Act rules</a>, venting refrigerant is illegal; recovered gas and old cabinets require documented disposal.</td>
            <td>“Includes EPA Section 608 recovery of old charge and haul-away of old equipment.”</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Copper Line Set Replacement</th>
            <td>Moving from <a href="/guides/r22-refrigerant/">R-22 mineral oil</a> to R-410A, R-454B, or R-32 POE synthetic oil risks oil contamination or undersized suction piping.</td>
            <td>“New insulated copper suction and liquid lines sized to spec (or approved flush if concealed).”</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Thermostat &amp; Low-Voltage Wire</th>
            <td>Heat pumps and two-stage equipment need 5 to 8 conductors that older 4-wire thermostat cables lack.</td>
            <td>“Thermostat model included plus new 18/8 cable pull if existing conductors fall short.”</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Ductwork &amp; Plenum Transitions</th>
            <td>Forcing a taller air handler onto leaky old plenums spikes static pressure and wears out blower motors early.</td>
            <td>“Custom sheet-metal supply/return transitions, mastic sealing, and filter rack included.”</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Worked Example: Comparing Two Bids Side by Side ($7,900 vs. $9,400)</h2>
    <div class="worked-example">
      <p>
        A homeowner with a 15-year-old R-22 3-ton split AC receives two quotes for the same nominal capacity:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Contractor A (“Quick Bid”):</strong> <strong>$7,900</strong> flat line: “Install 3-ton 14 SEER2 AC and coil.” No model numbers, no line set, and small print: “Permits and code upgrades billed separately.”</li>
        <li><strong>Contractor B (“Itemized Bid”):</strong> <strong>$9,400</strong> total, listing matched AHRI reference at 15.2 SEER2, new copper line set, 240V disconnect whip, sheet-metal return plenum with 4-inch filter cabinet, condensate float switch, EPA 608 recovery, municipal permit, and 2-year labor warranty.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
        <strong>The math check:</strong> Adding an omitted permit ($250), new line set ($750), disconnect/safety kit ($300), and plenum transition ($400) raises Contractor A’s $7,900 bid by <strong>$1,700</strong> to <strong>$9,600</strong>—making the “cheaper” bid <strong>$200 more expensive</strong> than Contractor B’s $9,400 scope.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Red Flags on Repair Quotes Too</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Zero part diagnosis:</strong> A line reading “Fix AC — $1,150” without naming the capacitor, contactor, blower motor, TXV valve, or leak site prevents price verification.</li>
      <li><strong>“Top off refrigerant” without leak search:</strong> Sealed units do not burn refrigerant. Adding R-22 or R-410A without locating the pinhole leak wastes money.</li>
      <li><strong>Today-only cliff discounts:</strong> Claims that prices rise $1,500 if you wait 24 hours are sales urgency, not mechanical reality.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-list">
      {FAQ_ITEMS.map((item) => (
        <details>
          <summary>{item.question}</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">{item.answer}</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>

    <h2>Next Step: Run the Numbers</h2>
    <p>
      Once your quotes cover equal scope, enter both totals into the <a href="/">HVAC Repair or Replace Calculator</a> and review <a href="/guides/5000-rule/">the $5,000 and 50% rules</a> before approving work.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
